## Final report

## Reviewed
`HEAD` (branch tip) @ 8bc7eded2f4b0ea4f940d16a09c118fcc7f4b536, "pricing: simpler split_bill". One file changed: `tinyshop/pricing.py` (+3/−6). I got the diff with `git diff HEAD~1 HEAD`. The working tree is clean: `git status --porcelain` printed nothing.

## Findings (reproduced)
### F1: `split_bill` shares no longer add up to the total
Where: `tinyshop/pricing.py:5-6`

The new code rounds once (`share = round(total_cents / people)`) and gives everyone that same share. Whenever the total doesn't divide evenly, the shares miss the total: they come up short when the result rounds down and go over when it rounds up.

Command:
    python3 -I -c "import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'.'); from tinyshop.pricing import split_bill
    for t,p in [(100,3),(200,3),(10,4),(99,1),(0,5)]:
        s=split_bill(t,p); print(t,p,s,sum(s),sum(s)==t)"
Output (exit 0):
    100 3 [33, 33, 33] 99 False
    200 3 [67, 67, 67] 201 False
    10 4 [2, 2, 2, 2] 8 False
    99 1 [99] 99 True
    0 5 [0, 0, 0, 0, 0] 0 True
Expected: `sum(split_bill(t, p)) == t` for every input. The docstring, which this commit kept, promises this, and so does the commit message. The parent commit got it right: `[34, 33, 33] [67, 67, 66] [3, 3, 2, 2]`.
Fix: Revert to `base, rem = divmod(total_cents, people); return [base + 1 if i < rem else base for i in range(people)]`. Also add a test with an uneven split, for example `split_bill(100, 3) == [34, 33, 33]` or `sum(...) == 100`.

